Yes — but I’d change one crucial variable in your mechanism.

What you’re describing is essentially counterfactual war as arbitration: instead of physically executing a conflict, both sides commit their available capabilities, objectives, constraints, alliances, doctrines, and intended moves simultaneously — one, two, three, shoot — and an adjudication system runs the counterfactual. That general direction is no longer science fiction. NATO explicitly uses modeling, simulation, data analytics and AI to explore military options, and has been experimenting with generative AI in strategic wargames. CSIS has also run repeated conflict simulations specifically because one run is not enough to characterize an uncertain conflict. 

I think the strongest version of your idea is not:

\text{simulation winner} \Rightarrow \text{gets disputed territory}

It is:

\text{counterfactual war}
\Rightarrow
\text{credible distribution of outcomes/costs}
\Rightarrow
\text{bargaining update}
\Rightarrow
\text{peaceful settlement}

That’s a subtle change, but it solves a huge problem.

The “one, two, three, shoot” part is powerful

Normally, international bargaining has a nasty information problem.

Russia doesn’t know exactly what NATO would do. NATO doesn’t know exactly what Russia would do. Each side doesn’t completely know the other’s red lines, reserves, willingness to absorb casualties, political endurance, allies’ behavior, etc.

And both have incentives to bluff.

Your simultaneous reveal is basically a commit-reveal protocol:

C_A = H(plan_A)

C_B = H(plan_B)

Both sides cryptographically commit before seeing the other’s play.

Then:

1 — 2 — 3 — reveal.

Now neither can rewrite its opening strategy after discovering what the opponent chose.

That really does import the useful property of rock-paper-scissors.

But don’t ask the AI “Who wins?”

Ask it:

P(outcome\mid A,B,world)

Run thousands or millions of variants.

Maybe the result is:

Outcome	Estimated probability
A achieves objectives	23%
B achieves objectives	31%
negotiated/stalemate	38%
uncontrolled escalation	8%

Alongside:

* expected casualties
* civilian casualties
* infrastructure destruction
* economic losses
* displacement
* duration
* probability of alliance expansion
* probability of regime instability
* nuclear-escalation risk
* reconstruction cost
* postwar political outcome

Now suddenly “winning” becomes much harder to bullshit yourself about.

That’s actually consistent with existing wargaming evidence: simulations often reveal that nominal victory can still be catastrophic. CSIS’s Taiwan games, for example, frequently produced a successful defense while simultaneously producing enormous military and economic losses. Their later nuclear games produced nuclear escalation in some iterations and emphasized the importance of diplomatic off-ramps rather than treating victory as binary. 

That’s your lose-lose observation made quantitative.

