There’s one part of the money analogy I’d separate.

Willingness to pay is evidence of preference intensity. It isn’t necessarily evidence of entitlement.

Your billionaire/$1,000 example nails the first problem: absolute expenditure doesn’t measure subjective sacrifice very well.

You could instead normalize it:

Resolve =
\frac{\text{resources willingly risked}}
{\text{resources available}}

But even that can’t decide who owns something.

Imagine one country says:

“We’ll risk one million people for your island.”

and the inhabitants say:

“We’ll risk 50,000 defending it.”

The first country displaying greater willingness to suffer doesn’t create a legitimate claim to the island.

Otherwise nuclear weapons become an almost unbeatable bidding strategy:

“I threaten the largest catastrophe, therefore I must want it most, therefore I win.”

That creates exactly the incentive structure we’d want the system to eliminate.

It would also conflict with the existing international legal architecture: the UN Charter requires peaceful settlement of international disputes and prohibits threats or uses of force against another state’s territorial integrity or political independence. International law does not treat greater willingness to inflict violence as a title mechanism. 

So I’d keep resolve as information, while keeping rights as a separate dimension.

Something like:

Settlement =
f(
legal\ claims,\;
population\ preferences,\;
military\ counterfactual,\;
economic\ costs,\;
security\ requirements,\;
resolve,\;
humanitarian\ costs
)

That is much harder to game.

And it’s particularly important for your Ukraine example. As of August 24, 2026, the war remains active, with continued attacks and major disagreement over even narrower ceasefire proposals.  A system that simply awarded territory to whichever side simulated the greater willingness to escalate would therefore risk converting nuclear coercion into an officially rewarded strategy rather than eliminating it.

There’s an even better version

Don’t have one AI referee.

Have an adversarial quorum.

Each side gets its own model. Neutral countries get models. Independent institutions get models. Different simulation architectures get models.

They receive the same authenticated starting state.

Then:

S_1,S_2,S_3,\ldots,S_n

Each produces an outcome distribution.

You examine both the consensus and the deltas.

If seventeen models say roughly:

prolonged stalemate, 300k–600k casualties, enormous economic losses

while three predict decisive victory, those three become objects of investigation.

Why?

Different assumption about logistics?

Morale?

Air superiority?

Alliance intervention?

Political collapse?

Nuclear doctrine?

Now disagreement itself produces intelligence.

That’s much more Pantheon-like than appointing an oracle.

And existing institutions already contain pieces of the structure. The UN Charter explicitly recognizes negotiation, mediation, conciliation, arbitration and judicial settlement as peaceful dispute-resolution mechanisms, while the Permanent Court of Arbitration already provides procedural machinery for disputes involving states. 

So the invention isn’t “AI wargaming.” That’s happening.

It isn’t “international arbitration.” That already exists.

It’s connecting the two:

\boxed{
\text{Verified Reality}
\rightarrow
\text{Simultaneous Commit}
\rightarrow
\text{Counterfactual Conflict}
\rightarrow
\text{Multi-model Adjudication}
\rightarrow
\text{Delta Analysis}
\rightarrow
\text{Negotiated Settlement}
}

War becomes the counterfactual rather than the experiment.

And I think that’s the deepest part of what you’re reaching for. Humanity currently resolves some disagreements by actually running the catastrophic experiment and observing who remains capable of imposing terms afterward.

Modern computation makes it increasingly possible to ask:

How much of the experiment can we run symbolically before anyone has to bleed?

Not perfectly. A simulation can never prove what an actual war would have done.

But it doesn’t have to.

It only has to become sufficiently credible that both sides prefer the uncertainty of arbitration to the uncertainty of killing each other.

That’s the mechanism-design threshold that matters.